# Why `\n` Fails in PHP Email Clients and How to Fix It

In legacy PHP applications, particularly those utilizing older frameworks or custom email classes, developers frequently encounter a subtle but persistent formatting issue. As seen in typical scenarios involving bid notifications or transactional messages, inserting a literal newline character using `\n` within the message string often results in a single block of text when rendered in the recipient's inbox.

Consider a standard implementation where a raw string is constructed for an email body:

```php
$message = "Good news! The item# $item_number on which you placed a bid of \$ $bid_price is now available for purchase at your bid price.\n";
$message .= "The seller, $bid_user is making this offer.\n";
// ... additional content
$htmlContent = $baseClass->email_friend(..., $message, ...);
```

When the script executes and `echo $message;` is run in a browser or CLI environment, the output appears on separate lines as expected. This works because PHP treats `\n` as a standard escape sequence representing a line feed during string interpolation and output buffering. However, once that string is passed to an email transport layer, the behavior changes completely. The newline characters vanish, leaving the entire message compressed into one paragraph.

The root cause lies in how SMTP protocols and email clients handle raw text versus HTML. Most legacy PHP email wrappers default to `Content-Type: text/plain`. While `\n` works for standard console output or file logging, many mail transfer agents (MTAs) and client applications ignore lone line feeds in plain text emails unless they are part of a CRLF sequence (`\r\n`). However, the more significant issue usually stems from the fact that these older libraries often strip formatting characters entirely to ensure compatibility with strict spam filters or legacy parsers.

To resolve this without upgrading the entire architecture to `PHPMailer` or `SwiftMailer`, you must explicitly instruct the email client to render the message as HTML. Even if the content contains simple text, changing the content type allows the email client to interpret standard newline characters correctly within the body of the message.

You can achieve this by modifying the parameters passed to your custom email class, assuming it supports an optional MIME type flag or by adjusting the underlying headers directly before calling the method:

```php
// Ensure the library knows we are sending HTML-compatible text
$headers = "From: no-reply@example.com\r\n";
$headers .= "MIME-Version: 1.0\r\n";
$headers .= "Content-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8\r\n";

// Pass the headers if your email_friend method accepts them, 
// or ensure the internal logic sets Content-Type to HTML.

$message = "Good news! The item# $item_number on which you placed a bid of \$ $bid_price is now available for purchase at your bid price.\n
The seller, $bid_user is making this offer.\n";

// If the library allows header injection:
$email_data = [
    'to' => $email,
    'subject' => 'Bid Successful',
    'body' => $message,
    'headers' => $headers 
];

$htmlContent = $baseClass->email_friend($item_number, $title, $bid_price, $bid_user, $mcat, $message, $email, $VIEWSTATE, $EVENTVALIDATION, $URL);
```

By setting the `Content-Type` to `text/html`, you signal to the mail client that the line breaks are intentional. In many email clients, this also enables the rendering of paragraphs if you add `<br>` tags or use `<p>` blocks, though for simple text emails, the `\n` usually suffices once the content type is honored.

Another critical pitfall involves the carriage return character (`\r`). While modern systems often handle `\n` adequately in HTML mode, strict adherence to SMTP standards dictates that line endings should be `CRLF` (`\r\n`). If your email library strictly adheres to RFC 5322, replacing `\n` with `"\r\n"` within the string literal might provide a more robust solution, particularly if the underlying MTA is known for being strict about line folding.

```php
$message = "Good news! The item# $item_number...\r\nThe seller, $bid_user...";
```

If you cannot modify the library's internal logic or headers, the safest fallback is to replace `\n` with `<br>` tags when using HTML content types. This approach guarantees consistency across Outlook, Gmail, and mobile clients, as they all respect standard HTML paragraph breaks regardless of the underlying line feed characters. The trade-off here is that you lose the flexibility of pure text formatting, but for bid notifications where layout precision matters less than readability, this is often the preferred engineering decision.
